When Marta Lindqvist was asked to give a toast at her sister’s wedding, she spent three weeks rehearsing in the shower and the last hour before the reception hiding in the venue’s cloakroom. The toast itself lasted barely two minutes. Nobody else remembered a word of it. Marta remembered every second.

{{41}}

The explanation, she discovered, is an ancient one. Faced with what it takes to be danger, the body releases adrenaline: the heart races, the mouth dries and the hands begin to shake. The trouble is that the brain cannot easily tell the difference between a hungry predator and a hundred expectant faces.

{{42}}

What finally made a difference was a small experiment she ran with a group of colleagues. Before delivering a short talk, half of them were asked to repeat the words “I am calm” to themselves; the other half were told to repeat “I am excited”.

{{43}}

Marta is careful not to overstate the finding, which came from a modest sample and a single afternoon.

{{44}}

But technique alone, she is quick to point out, is not enough. The rest is repetition, and it is here that her workshops differ most from the glossy courses promising instant confidence.

{{45}}

The method works, if slowly. Most participants report that their nerves never disappear altogether, and those who arrive hoping to be cured, Marta says, usually leave slightly disappointed and, a little later, rather relieved.

{{46}}
